            question: (
                id: "motor-gain",
                title: "How is a motor's tracking rate passed to avian?",
                source: "brief 4b-4 Measured (motors)",
                description: r#"avian 0.8's velocity motor changes the relative speed by `damping x error` per substep, so its `damping` is a per-substep gain, stable only below about 2. The authored default 30 (documented as 1/s) was passed through unconverted: a wheel pinned at its centre did not turn, a 10 cm one spun at 400 rad/s, and arms tracked only by luck."#,
                decision: true,
                options: [
                    (
                        label: "Rate (1/s) times substep, capped at 1",
                        caption: r#"Recommended. Keep the authored field as a rate; the seam converts it with the substep length and caps the gain at dead-beat (1), so every rate is stable. Default rate 360/s = gain 1 at the default 6 substeps at 60 Hz. Saved motors keep their field; new ones save 360."#,
                    ),
                    (
                        label: "Author the per-substep gain",
                        caption: r#"Expose avian's number directly, clamped to (0, 1]. Simpler mapping, but the feel changes with the substep setting and the number means nothing outside the solver."#,
                    ),
                ],
            ),
            answer: (
