                title: "Which tolerances do the contour area and CSG properties use?",
                source: "Geometry property tests (docs/briefs/6-2-geometry-proptests.md)",
                description: r#"polygonize bisects every contour vertex onto the zero set, so the errors come from three places only: simplify_ring drops vertices within 0.05 cell of their chord, a curve keeps one-cell chords (sag cell^2/8R), and the grid chamfers a corner. CSG makes corners anywhere along a boundary and drops slivers under 4 cell^2 on purpose."#,
                decision: true,
                options: [
                    (
                        label: "Derived from the cell size",
                        caption: r#"Bare leaves: exact, or the inscribed 48-gon loss (0.29 % of the disc) for circles and caps. Contoured leaf: 0.05 cell x perimeter + (corners + 2) cell^2. CSG areas: 0.25 cell x perimeter + 4 cell^2 per component, summed over the areas a relation uses. Field classification: points within 1.5 cells of the boundary are not judged. Submerged clip: 1e-4 relative plus the f32 shoelace rounding bound. Mirror field: 1e-4 (1 + |p|)."#,
                    ),
                    (
                        label: "A flat percentage",
                        caption: r#"Simpler to read, but too loose for large shapes and too tight for thin rods (a 10 cm rod at a 2.8 cm cell is off by 1.3 % within the contour's design)."#,
                    ),
                ],
            ),
            answer: (
